437
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:54,039
and the pace there is actually super fast.

438
00:17:54,040 --> 00:17:56,999
You have to push out tons of information.

439
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:58,879
But actually, we're not really trying to

440
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:00,999
show a livestream sales host on stage.

441
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:02,159
Right, that's the part

442
00:18:02,160 --> 00:18:03,640
I felt I didn't handle well.

443
00:18:06,120 --> 00:18:08,320
Thank you, teachers, for your hard work.

444
00:18:10,280 --> 00:18:12,160
Big Bro's Mini Classroom is in session.

445
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:16,159
Idiom fill-in-the-blank, your teacher taught you this.

446
00:18:16,160 --> 00:18:18,360
Come on, Big Bro's got a question for you.

447
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:24,079
Nüwa mended what?

448
00:18:24,080 --> 00:18:24,800
Right.

449
00:18:24,801 --> 00:18:26,280
The one from that mythology story.

450
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:28,120
Think carefully.

451
00:18:28,121 --> 00:18:29,799
Nüwa mended what?

452
00:18:29,800 --> 00:18:31,879
Nüwa mended...

453
00:18:31,880 --> 00:18:33,799
How could Nüwa be mending calcium?

454
00:18:33,800 --> 00:18:35,119
Oh, Nüwa mended the sky.

455
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:37,359
Would Nüwa even need you for calcium?

456
00:18:37,360 --> 00:18:38,040
Forget it.

457
00:18:38,041 --> 00:18:39,440
Next question, come on.

458
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:45,599
What's the opposite of naive?

459
00:18:45,600 --> 00:18:46,600
This one's easy.

460
00:18:46,601 --> 00:18:47,639
Think carefully.

461
00:18:47,640 --> 00:18:49,319
What's the opposite of naive?

462
00:18:49,320 --> 00:18:50,000
Evil.

463
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:50,720
'Evian'.

464
00:18:50,721 --> 00:18:53,799
How can the opposite of naive be 'Evian'?

465
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:56,119
Then isn't it 'hypocritical' or 'worldly'?

466
00:18:56,120 --> 00:18:57,840
Where did 'evian' even come from?

467
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:02,920
Why are you crying?

468
00:19:03,560 --> 00:19:04,799
Big Bro didn't mean to yell at you.

469
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:05,800
Don't cry.

470
00:19:06,600 --> 00:19:07,120
Okay, how about this.

471
00:19:07,121 --> 00:19:08,841
Big Bro's gonna get you a candy, wait here.

472
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:13,479
Big Bro remembers you love candy.

473
00:19:13,480 --> 00:19:14,519
Mandarin flavor.

474
00:19:14,520 --> 00:19:15,360
No.

475
00:19:15,360 --> 00:19:15,960
Orange, then.

476
00:19:15,960 --> 00:19:16,280
No.

477
00:19:16,280 --> 00:19:17,120
Grape? No.

478
00:19:17,120 --> 00:19:17,800
Strawberry?

479
00:19:17,801 --> 00:19:19,920
Not that either. Then what do you want?

480
00:19:21,080 --> 00:19:22,679
So I have to pull out the big move, huh?

481
00:19:22,680 --> 00:19:23,680
Fine.

482
00:19:24,840 --> 00:19:26,199
Big Bro's gonna do a kip-up for you.

483
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:27,080
Watch closely.

484
00:19:27,081 --> 00:19:29,560
I feel like my performance was too serious.

485
00:19:36,960 --> 00:19:38,320
My butt.

486
00:19:39,080 --> 00:19:40,999
You're laughing? You're still laughing?

487
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:42,480
What are you laughing at!

488
00:19:43,720 --> 00:19:44,720
And the teacher was laughing.

489
00:19:44,720 --> 00:19:45,280
The teachers were laughing.

490
00:19:45,281 --> 00:19:46,401
He's cussing at the teacher.

491
00:19:49,240 --> 00:19:50,320
It's over.

492
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:54,439
I really like it.

493
00:19:54,440 --> 00:19:56,359
First of all, I think the character really came through.

494
00:19:56,360 --> 00:19:58,999
So in everyday life, are you really that doting on your little sister?

495
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:00,639
Or do you two also roughhouse,

496
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:01,560
or something like that?

497
00:20:01,561 --> 00:20:03,159
Because there's a pretty big age gap between us,

498
00:20:03,160 --> 00:20:05,359
so mostly I spoil her.

499
00:20:05,360 --> 00:20:06,759
But when tutoring her on problems,

500
00:20:06,760 --> 00:20:08,399
sometimes she really annoys me too.

501
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:09,999
So I just snap at her a couple times, that's all.

502
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:10,880
So are you usually

503
00:20:10,881 --> 00:20:11,999
a pretty humorous person?

504
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:13,519
Yes, I think I'm pretty good

505
00:20:13,520 --> 00:20:14,719
at deadpan humor.

506
00:20:14,720 --> 00:20:15,799
I like it. Yes.

507
00:20:15,800 --> 00:20:17,119
But you have one issue:

508
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:19,079
Tongue-tip consonants when you speak.

509
00:20:19,080 --> 00:20:21,399
Every sound made with the tip of your tongue

510
00:20:21,400 --> 00:20:22,040
is a problem.

511
00:20:22,041 --> 00:20:23,279
We'll work on that gradually later.

512
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:24,520
Okay.

513
00:20:26,880 --> 00:20:27,880
Thank you, teachers.

514
00:20:44,320 --> 00:20:45,679
So it turns out

515
00:20:45,680 --> 00:20:48,320
I've always been an obedient puppet.

516
00:20:49,320 --> 00:20:54,879
Countless threads I couldn't see clearly wrapped around my body.

517
00:20:54,880 --> 00:20:57,760
Every move other people made was pulling at my every feeling.

518
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:03,399
But right now,

519
00:21:03,400 --> 00:21:05,479
but right now, I don't want this.

520
00:21:05,480 --> 00:21:06,600
I don't want this anymore.

521
00:21:10,200 --> 00:21:12,199
Starting from this moment,

522
00:21:12,200 --> 00:21:12,960
I refuse to be bound

523
00:21:12,961 --> 00:21:15,599
by these threads any longer.

524
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:17,039
They were never my burden,

525
00:21:17,040 --> 00:21:18,919
never my obstacle.

526
00:21:18,920 --> 00:21:19,999
I will no longer give in,

527
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:21,359
no longer people-please.

528
00:21:21,360 --> 00:21:23,359
These so-called imperfections are not my flaws.

529
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:25,840
They are proof that I've cried, laughed, and lived, vividly.

530
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:27,920
The script of my life,

531
00:21:27,921 --> 00:21:29,959
I read it long ago in 'Heaven'.

532
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:31,479
The reason I chose this script

533
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:32,440
is because in this life

534
00:21:32,441 --> 00:21:34,679
there are things I believe are worth it.

535
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:35,719
So,

536
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:38,199
I want to accept all my sensitivity and inner turmoil.

537
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:39,959
I don't think they're burdens,

538
00:21:39,960 --> 00:21:41,879
but gifts heaven gave me,

539
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:43,840
what makes my life's script special.

540
00:21:47,840 --> 00:21:49,199
Finally,

541
00:21:49,200 --> 00:21:51,000
I want to say out loud to myself:

542
00:21:52,680 --> 00:21:55,079
I'm doing fine.

543
00:21:55,080 --> 00:21:57,559
I can do this.

544
00:21:57,560 --> 00:22:00,039
I want to be one of a kind,

545
00:22:00,040 --> 00:22:03,440
a version of me that shines.

546
00:22:07,480 --> 00:22:08,440
Let me give you some advice:

547
00:22:08,441 --> 00:22:09,719
next time on this show,

548
00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:12,519
try the stand-up comedy approach,

549
00:22:12,520 --> 00:22:14,159
making fun of yourself.

550
00:22:14,160 --> 00:22:15,919
To make it more like a comedy?

551
00:22:15,920 --> 00:22:17,759
Because that's more powerful.

552
00:22:17,760 --> 00:22:19,239
People who dare to laugh at themselves are the bravest...

553
00:22:19,240 --> 00:22:20,399
more courageous people.

554
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:21,600
Just don't play along with it.

555
00:22:22,040 --> 00:22:22,720
Okay.

556
00:22:22,721 --> 00:22:24,759
I think in the future I could try comedy,

557
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:26,279
because I've never tried it before.

558
00:22:26,280 --> 00:22:28,439
This line was limiting you.

559
00:22:28,440 --> 00:22:29,599
Right, exactly.

560
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:30,959
What earned you praise is also

561
00:22:30,960 --> 00:22:32,439
what drew the criticism.

562
00:22:32,440 --> 00:22:34,959
At least you held on, even though inside,

563
00:22:34,960 --> 00:22:36,839
at that moment, you were already breaking.

564
00:22:36,840 --> 00:22:37,440
I was okay.

565
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:38,280
You were okay?

566
00:22:38,280 --> 00:22:39,280
Yes, I held on.

567
00:22:39,281 --> 00:22:40,599
But honestly,

568
00:22:40,600 --> 00:22:41,679
at that moment,

569
00:22:41,680 --> 00:22:43,759
the audience was anxious for you.

570
00:22:43,760 --> 00:22:44,839
So at that point, you'd already...

571
00:22:44,840 --> 00:22:46,519
